  "limitations": [
    "Database transactions cannot make external provider effects atomic and do not provide a general compensation workflow. ORCH-06 fences internal authority and recovery only.",
    "Cancellation after call-start is best effort and cannot recall an already-sent request. Authoritative successful receipts remain successful and visible after cancellation or a sibling terminal denial.",
    "Generic READ retries and RUN-05 WRITE delivery or reconciliation attempts deliberately use separate durable paths. ORCH-06 does not claim provider exactly-once effects.",
    "Tests use deterministic adapters, injected clocks, and file-backed SQLite services rather than a production worker loop, live provider, distributed rate-limit load, or multi-region environment.",
    "Rate limiting is durable fixed-window and template-scoped only. No token bucket, cross-region fairness, or live policy-revocation claim is made.",
    "Schema IDs and payload budgets are sealed, but full runtime JSON-Schema output validation, artifact persistence, and a complete attempt/output operator timeline remain SAFE-06, RUN-06, and AC-14 composition work.",
    "The WRITE dispatcher bounds the action result projection. Connector-owned durable receipt storage may retain a larger JSON-safe metadata record before dispatcher reconciliation; receipt-store quotas and retention remain adapter and RUN-06 hardening scope.",
    "Audit context pseudonymizes provenance but does not authenticate cancellation callers. API and worker composition must derive cancellation identity from server-owned authentication.",
    "No Alembic migration is added. PostgreSQL DDL compilation is covered, but PostgreSQL runtime locking, contention, and parity are not executed here.",
    "A concrete registry-backed connector READ adapter is covered. A production model adapter and live token-provider integration remain adapter-composition work."
  ]
